.\" Manpage for the dir_hash C++ library.
.TH DIR_HASH 3 "May 2026" "dir-hash 0.1.0" "C++ Library Functions"
.SH NAME
dir_hash::hash_directory \- content-based directory tree hash with BLAKE3
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include "dir_hash.hpp"
.PP
.B namespace dir_hash {
.B inline namespace v1 {
.PP
.BI "template <typename " Fn ", typename " ErrFn " = detail::NoopError>"
.BI "std::array<uint8_t, 32> hash_directory("
.BI "    std::string_view " path ,
.BI "    " Fn "&& " callback ,
.BI "    " ErrFn "&& " on_error " = {},"
.BI "    std::pmr::memory_resource* " mem
.BI "        = std::pmr::get_default_resource());"
.PP
.B }}
.fi
.PP
Header-only. Link against
.B \-lblake3 .
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR hash_directory ()
walks the tree rooted at
.I path
with
.BR fts (3),
hashes every regular file with BLAKE3, and computes a Merkle-style hash
for every directory by feeding the lexicographically-sorted multiset of
child hashes into BLAKE3. The directory hash is name-agnostic: two
directories whose files have identical content \(em regardless of
filenames or arrangement \(em hash to the same value. Symlinks and
other non-regular entries are excluded. An empty directory hashes to
BLAKE3 of an empty input.
.PP
.I callback
is invoked once per node as its hash is finalised, with signature:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
void(std::string_view path,
     const std::array<uint8_t, 32>& hash,
     bool is_dir);
.fi
.RE
.PP
The
.I path
passed to the callback is valid only for the duration of the call;
copy it if it must outlive the callback.
.PP
.I on_error
is invoked for files or directories that cannot be read, with
signature:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
void(std::string_view path, std::error_code ec);
.fi
.RE
.PP
The offending node is skipped and does not contribute to its parent's
hash. The default is a no-op.
.PP
.I mem
governs all internal allocations \(em path buffer, child-hash
accumulators, and the 4 MiB read buffer. It does not affect
caller-side allocations.
.SH "I/O BEHAVIOUR"
Every file is opened with
.B POSIX_FADV_SEQUENTIAL
and closed with
.BR POSIX_FADV_DONTNEED ,
which avoids polluting the page cache. Files are read in 4 MiB chunks
and fed to the BLAKE3 hasher incrementally.
.SH "RETURN VALUE"
The 32-byte hash of the directory at
.IR path .
.SH ERRORS
.BR hash_directory ()
throws
.B std::system_error
if
.I path
cannot be opened or is not a directory. The contained
.B std::error_code
wraps the underlying
.I errno
under
.BR std::system_category .
.PP
Per-file errors during traversal are reported via
.IR on_error ;
the traversal continues.
.SH EXAMPLES
Root hash only:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
auto root = dir_hash::hash_directory(
    "/backup/2024-01",
    [](std::string_view, const std::array<uint8_t, 32>&, bool) {});
.fi
.RE
.PP
With a monotonic arena:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
std::pmr::monotonic_buffer_resource arena(64 * 1024);
auto root = dir_hash::hash_directory(
    "/backup/2024-01",
    [](std::string_view, const std::array<uint8_t, 32>&, bool) {},
    {},
    &arena);
.fi
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR dirhash (1),
.BR fts (3),
.BR posix_fadvise (2)
.SH STANDARDS
The library targets C++17.
.SH AUTHORS
Keith Williams.
